---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import VideoGrid from '../components/VideoGrid.astro';
import Video from '../components/Video.astro';

// ── HOW TO ADD A VIDEO ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// `vimeo` is the id from the URL, or 'id/hash' for a private link, or ''
// for a "Coming soon" tile. Only REAL clients. No invented names.
//
// Note: this page used to lead with Vimeo 1192295930 as a generic "Featured
// Reel" for recurring content. That is the Nolensville Dance Company intro,
// which now lives on /dance where it belongs, so it was removed rather than
// stand in for business work.
//
// STILL COMING (deliberately NOT placeholder tiles: three real videos read
// better than five tiles where two say "Coming soon"):
//   - CBSR owner weekly edit  (the flagship recurring-series proof)
//   - A podcast example. Podcast production is a headline service on the
//     homepage and nothing on this page demonstrates it yet.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const videos = [
  // NOTE: the Coldwell Banker agent interview (1214149996) is deliberately NOT
  // here. It is embedded in the "How it works" section above as the proof for
  // the interview engine. Never the same video twice on one page.
  { title: 'Webb School Summer Camp', category: 'Education', vimeo: '1214150712' },
  // Vimeo titles this "Church at Nolensville Crud War" (a youth event).
  { title: 'Church at Nolensville', category: 'Community event', vimeo: '1214151209' },
  // Made for Mobile IV Stat, which was Geoff's own company and has since closed,
  // with Geoff himself on camera. Kept because the work is good, but labelled by
  // what it IS rather than who it was for: naming a company he owned would be a
  // false client credit, and naming a closed one invites a prospect to look up a
  // business that no longer exists. The section heading is "What the work looks
  // like", not "clients", so a work-type label is honest here.
  // Vimeo titles this "Tutoring Rocks.m4v". Approved client; the blog describes
  // it as a brand video shot on site at their Nolensville location.
  { title: 'Patient welcome video', category: 'Healthcare', vimeo: '926434787' },
  { title: 'Tutoring Rocks', category: 'Brand video', vimeo: '707958942' },
];
---
<Layout title="Business Video Production Nashville | SDub Media" description="Interview-led video content for Nashville businesses: one sit-down becomes a quarter of content. Weekly series, on-camera reels, headshots, events and brand films. Retainer-friendly.">
<section>
  <div class="container-narrow text-center">
    <span class="eyebrow">For businesses</span>
    <h1>Video that runs on a schedule,<br>not a one-time hope.</h1>
    <p style="font-size: 1.1rem; margin-top: 1.5rem;">Interview-led series, on-camera reels, headshot days, events and brand films. Photography too, from the same people. We run the production system so your team does not have to.</p>
    <div style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary">Start a conversation</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{/* The interview engine. This is the mechanism behind the retainer pitch and
    it was missing from the site entirely: one sit-down becomes months of
    content, which is what makes "every week" possible without eating the
    client's calendar. The 19 and 16 figures are delivered work, confirmed
    publishable by Geoff 2026-07-29. The recruitment series is deliberately
    NOT shown or described in detail: that footage cannot be published. */}
<section style="background: var(--soft);">
  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">How it works</span>
      <h2>You sit down once. You show up every week.</h2>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size: 1.1rem;">Most businesses give up on video because of the calendar, not the budget. Nobody has time to be filmed every week, so it stops after a month.</p>
    <p style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">So we do it the other way round.</p>
    <ul class="facts">
      <li><strong>Two or three hours</strong> of filming. That is your entire part</li>
      <li>A run of <strong>interviews</strong>, usually the owner or whoever knows the business best</li>
      <li>Cut into <strong>a quarter's worth</strong> of short pieces</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">The editing is ours, which is why we keep editors on the team rather than a waiting list. You are on camera for an afternoon and visible for a quarter.</p>
    <!-- This was buried inside the list above as a bullet, which broke the
         list's rhythm and clipped the second half to "We did sixteen for
         another." It is the page's strongest proof; it gets to stand alone. -->
    <p style="margin-top: 1.25rem; font-size: 1.1rem;">One broker sat down with his top agents for a single day. That session became <strong>nineteen pieces of content</strong>. A second broker's day produced sixteen.</p>
    <div style="margin-top: 2rem;">
      <Video id="1214149996" title="Agent on camera, Coldwell Banker Southern Realty" />
      <p style="font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 0.6rem;">One cut from an agent interview day. Coldwell Banker Southern Realty.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <img class="wedding-hero-img" src="/images/corporate/bts-bar-shoot.jpg"
           alt="A brand shoot in progress: two large softboxes, a cinema camera and a second angle set up around a bar"
           loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1200" />
      <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 0.75rem;">A brand shoot mid-setup. Two lights, two angles, and a phone rolling for social, so one visit covers every format.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="background: var(--soft);">
  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Photography</span>
      <h2>Photos and video from the same shoot.</h2>
    </div>
    <p>Headshots, event photos and brand images on the same day, with the same people, without booking a second company. They also match. Hire a video crew and a photographer separately and you get two sets of images that clearly came from different places.</p>
    <p style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">We run monthly headshot days for Coldwell Banker Southern Realty, rotating through their offices so agents get an updated portrait without booking a studio. If you list property, <a href="/real-estate-photography">real estate photography</a> is its own service.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="container" style="margin-top: 3rem;">
    <div class="wedding-strip">
      <img src="/images/events/gala-room.jpg" alt="A gala ballroom set for dinner, seen from the balcony before guests arrive" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1069" />
      <img src="/images/events/gala-tables.jpg" alt="A long banquet table lit by candles running toward a lit stage" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1069" />
      <img src="/images/events/gala-place-setting.jpg" alt="A place setting with orchids, candlelight and a printed menu" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1069" />
    </div>
    <p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 1.5rem; color: var(--muted);">Ballet Ball Nashville. Event photography, shot the same night we covered it on video.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">See it in action</span>
      <h2>What the work looks like.</h2>
    </div>
    {/* center: 4 videos leaves a partial last row; centering it looks
        deliberate instead of left-aligned against an empty column */}
    <VideoGrid items={videos} center />
  </div>
</section>

<section style="background: var(--soft);">
  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Who shoots it</span>
      <h2>Specialists, not one person with a camera.</h2>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size: 1.1rem;">Most small production companies are one generalist who books help when the calendar gets tight. We are built the other way: people who do one thing constantly and are therefore fast at it.</p>
    {/* Roles only, never names or faces: the team is not willing to appear on
        the marketing site and that stays their call. These four roles are the
        confirmed set (see /about). Do not add a fifth to fill a page. */}
    <ul class="facts" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
      <li><strong>Two videographers</strong>, including the owner, who has done every job the company sells</li>
      <li>A photographer who covers <strong>live events</strong>, for the days a sit-down turns into a room full of people</li>
      <li>A photographer who shoots <strong>homes only</strong>, if you list property as well</li>
      <li><strong>Editing in house</strong>, so your content never sits in someone else's queue</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">More on <a href="/about">who shows up</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">What it costs</span>
      <h2>Priced as a system, not per video.</h2>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size: 1.15rem;">Recurring content is a monthly arrangement rather than a per-video price, because the whole point is that it keeps running. One sitting turned into a quarter of content costs less per piece than any of those pieces would cost on its own.</p>
    <p style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">What moves the number:</p>
    <ul class="facts">
      <li><strong>How often we film.</strong> A quarterly sitting and a monthly one are different commitments</li>
      <li><strong>How many finished pieces</strong> you want out of each session, and in what formats</li>
      <li><strong>Whether photography rides along</strong> on the same day, which is cheaper than booking it separately</li>
      <li><strong>Whether it is a retainer or a one-off.</strong> Retainers get first call on the calendar</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">Tell us what you are trying to keep published and how often, and you will get a real number rather than a range.</p>
  </div>
</section>

{/* This page had exactly one CTA and it was at the very top. Anyone who read
    all the way down here is the warmest visitor the site gets, and the page
    handed them the footer. Every other service page already closed with one. */}
<section style="background: var(--soft);">
  <div class="container-narrow text-center">
    <span class="eyebrow">Start</span>
    <h2>Book the first sitting.</h2>
    <p style="font-size: 1.1rem; max-width: 520px; margin: 1.5rem auto 0;">Two or three hours of your time, once. We will tell you what we would make from it before you commit to anything.</p>
    <div style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary">Start a conversation</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</Layout>
